This is the final contents page (printed xiv in the header).

**19.3, How to build intelligence (printed 576, PDF p.600):** the kaleidoscope hypothesis; the essence of intelligence as abstraction acquisition and recombination; the importance of setting the right target; a new target — on-the-fly adaptation; the ARC Prize; the test-time adaptation era; and ARC-AGI 2.

**19.4, The missing ingredients: Search and symbols (printed 584, PDF p.608):** the two poles of abstraction; cognition as a combination of both kinds; why deep learning isn't a complete answer to abstraction generation; program synthesis as an alternative approach; blending deep learning and program synthesis; modular component recombination and lifelong learning; and the long-term vision.

**Chapter 20, Conclusions (printed 595, PDF p.619):**
- 20.1 key concepts in review — approaches to AI, what makes deep learning special within ML, how to think about deep learning, key enabling technologies, the universal ML workflow, and key network architectures;
- 20.2 limitations of deep learning; 20.3 what might lie ahead;
- 20.4 staying up to date — practicing on Kaggle, reading arXiv, exploring the Keras ecosystem;
- 20.5 final words.

The **index** starts on printed page 609.

How to use this page: 20.1 is a compact recap of the entire book, so it makes a good place to check your understanding after finishing (or to preview before starting). Sections 19.3-19.4 contain the authors' own perspective on the path toward general intelligence — read them as argument rather than established fact.
